{"kind":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Work with astronomical time and geographic observer locations."}],"sections":[],"seeAlsoSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/GettingStarted"],"anchor":"Essentials","title":"Essentials","generated":true}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"AstronomyKit uses "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/AstroTime","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" for time and "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/Observer","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" for geographic locations."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Working with Time","anchor":"Working-with-Time","type":"heading","level":2},{"text":"Creating Times","anchor":"Creating-Times","type":"heading","level":3},{"type":"codeListing","code":["\/\/ Current time","let now = AstroTime.now","","\/\/ From calendar components (in UTC)","let eclipse = AstroTime(year: 2024, month: 4, day: 8, hour: 18, minute: 15)","","\/\/ From Foundation Date","let time = AstroTime(Date())","","\/\/ From UT1 days since J2000 noon","let ut = AstroTime(ut: 9132.5)"],"syntax":"swift"},{"text":"Time Arithmetic","anchor":"Time-Arithmetic","type":"heading","level":3},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let now = AstroTime.now","","\/\/ Add days or hours","let tomorrow = now.addingDays(1)","let nextHour = now.addingHours(1)","let lastWeek = now.addingDays(-7)","","\/\/ Compare times","if tomorrow > now {","    print(\"Tomorrow comes after now\")","}"],"syntax":"swift"},{"text":"Converting to Date","anchor":"Converting-to-Date","type":"heading","level":3},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let time = AstroTime.now","let date: Date = time.date","","\/\/ Format for display","let formatter = DateFormatter()","formatter.dateStyle = .medium","formatter.timeStyle = .short","print(formatter.string(from: time.date))"],"syntax":"swift"},{"text":"Understanding UT vs TT","anchor":"Understanding-UT-vs-TT","type":"heading","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"AstroTime stores two time values internally. Universal Time (UT1) follows Earth’s rotation and drives rise and set times. Terrestrial Time (TT) is the uniform scale used for planetary orbits."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let time = AstroTime.now","print(\"UT: \\(time.universalTime)\")  \/\/ Days since J2000 noon (UT1)","print(\"TT: \\(time.terrestrialTime)\")  \/\/ Days since J2000 noon (TT)","","\/\/ Create from Terrestrial Time directly","let fromTT = AstroTime(tt: 9132.5)"],"syntax":"swift"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"AstronomyKit applies the difference (ΔT) for you.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Delta T Configuration","anchor":"Delta-T-Configuration","type":"heading","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The difference between TT and UT (ΔT) varies over time and comes from a model. AstronomyKit defaults to the Espenak-Meeus model. Set the model once at startup, before any other AstronomyKit call, if you want a different one:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["\/\/ Switch to the JPL Horizons Delta T model","AstronomyConfig.setDeltaTModel(.jplHorizons)","","\/\/ Query Delta T for a specific time","let deltaT = AstronomyConfig.deltaTEspenakMeeus(universalTime: 0)  \/\/ Seconds at J2000","","\/\/ Back to the default model","AstronomyConfig.setDeltaTModel(.espenakMeeus)"],"type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"code":"AstronomyConfig.reset()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" does not touch the Delta T model. It only frees the Pluto orbit cache.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Sidereal Time","anchor":"Sidereal-Time","type":"heading","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Get the sidereal time at the prime meridian:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let sidereal = AstroTime.now.siderealTime","print(\"Greenwich Sidereal Time: \\(sidereal) hours\")"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Calculate local sidereal time for any longitude:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let observer = Observer(latitude: 40.7, longitude: -74.0)  \/\/ NYC","let lst = AstroTime.now.siderealTime(longitude: observer.longitude)","print(\"Local Sidereal Time: \\(lst) hours\")"]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Working-with-Observers","text":"Working with Observers"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"anchor":"Creating-Observers","text":"Creating Observers"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["\/\/ Basic location (sea level)","let seattle = Observer(latitude: 47.6062, longitude: -122.3321)","","\/\/ With height above sea level (meters)","let everest = Observer(","    latitude: 27.9881,","    longitude: 86.9250,","    height: 8848.86",")"]},{"type":"heading","level":3,"anchor":"Coordinate-Conventions","text":"Coordinate Conventions"},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Latitude: -90° (south) to +90° (north)","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Longitude: -180° (west) to +180° (east)"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Height: meters above sea level"}]}]}]},{"anchor":"Built-in-Locations","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Built-in Locations"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let greenwich = Observer.greenwich       \/\/ Royal Observatory","let primeMeridian = Observer.primeMeridian  \/\/ 0°, 0°"],"syntax":"swift"},{"text":"Observer Properties","type":"heading","level":3,"anchor":"Observer-Properties"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let observer = Observer(latitude: 40.0, longitude: -105.0, height: 1600)","","\/\/ Local gravity (accounts for latitude and altitude)","let gravity = observer.gravity  \/\/ m\/s²","","\/\/ Atmospheric conditions at this elevation","let atmosphere = try observer.atmosphere","print(\"Pressure: \\(atmosphere.pressure) mbar\")"],"syntax":"swift"},{"text":"Observer Vectors","type":"heading","level":3,"anchor":"Observer-Vectors"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Get the observer’s position relative to Earth’s center:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let observer = Observer.greenwich","","\/\/ Position vector in AU","let position = try observer.vector(at: .now)","","\/\/ Full state (position and velocity)","let state = try observer.state(at: .now)","print(\"Position: \\(state.position)\")","print(\"Velocity: \\(state.velocity)\")"],"syntax":"swift"},{"text":"Time Zones","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Time-Zones"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"AstronomyKit works entirely in UTC. Convert to local time using Foundation:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let sunrise = try CelestialBody.sun.riseTime(after: .now, from: observer)!","","let formatter = DateFormatter()","formatter.timeZone = TimeZone(identifier: \"America\/New_York\")","formatter.timeStyle = .short","print(\"Sunrise (local): \\(formatter.string(from: sunrise.date))\")"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Codable Support","anchor":"Codable-Support"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Both types support "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Codable"},{"type":"text","text":" for persistence:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let observer = Observer(latitude: 40.7, longitude: -74.0)","let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(observer)","","let time = AstroTime.now","let timeData = try JSONEncoder().encode(time)"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Civil time and numerical compatibility","anchor":"Civil-time-and-numerical-compatibility"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AstroTime(Date)"},{"type":"text","text":", calendar components and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".now"},{"type":"text","text":" use civil UTC. From 1961 onward, a bundled USNO table converts UTC to TT, including the rate adjustments before 1972. Future dates retain the last announced leap-second offset. Before 1961, civil dates use the engine’s historical UT1 proxy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"universalTime"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"init(ut:)"},{"type":"text","text":" are modeled UT1 coordinates, not UTC timestamps. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"terrestrialTime"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"init(tt:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" are TT. Native search results already carry both scales; their ","type":"text"},{"code":".date","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" uses the same civil inverse as every other time. ","type":"text"},{"code":"addingDays","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"addingHours","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" add UT1 coordinate intervals. For civil calendar arithmetic, use Foundation and construct a new ","type":"text"},{"code":"AstroTime","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" from the resulting date.","type":"text"}]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Foundation cannot represent leap seconds. TT instants in positive UTC gaps map to the next transition; negative historical steps choose the later civil occurrence. Those instants cannot round-trip through ","type":"text"},{"code":"Date","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". Positive gaps in the selected Delta T model similarly map TT to the first UT1 coordinate after the jump. At negative overlaps, fixed-point iteration returns the first solution reached from its initial "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ut = tt"},{"text":" estimate. TT remains exact in memory, but numeric Codable remains UT1-based, so decoding a gap-clamped value derives TT again from the selected model. The table’s future convention and the modeled UT1 values do not establish future UTC or Earth-orientation accuracy. Review serialized times and invalidate version-dependent cached calculations when upgrading.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"metadata":{"title":"Time and Observers","roleHeading":"Article","modules":[{"name":"AstronomyKit"}],"role":"article"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit"]]},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/astronomykit\/timeandobservers"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/TimeAndObservers","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"references":{"doc://AstronomyKit/documentation/AstronomyKit/AstroTime":{"url":"\/documentation\/astronomykit\/astrotime","title":"AstroTime","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/AstroTime","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Represents a moment in time for astronomical calculations."}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"AstroTime","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"AstroTime"}]},"doc://AstronomyKit/documentation/AstronomyKit":{"role":"collection","identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Swift bindings for Don Cross’ "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"https:\/\/github.com\/cosinekitty\/astronomy","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" library."}],"kind":"symbol","title":"AstronomyKit","url":"\/documentation\/astronomykit","type":"topic"},"https://github.com/cosinekitty/astronomy":{"identifier":"https:\/\/github.com\/cosinekitty\/astronomy","type":"link","title":"Astronomy Engine","titleInlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Astronomy Engine"}],"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/cosinekitty\/astronomy"},"doc://AstronomyKit/documentation/AstronomyKit/Observer":{"title":"Observer","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A geographic location for observing celestial objects."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/Observer","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Observer","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Observer","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/astronomykit\/observer"},"doc://AstronomyKit/documentation/AstronomyKit/GettingStarted":{"type":"topic","kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/astronomykit\/gettingstarted","title":"Getting Started with AstronomyKit","identifier":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/GettingStarted","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Learn how to add AstronomyKit to your project and perform basic astronomical calculations."}],"role":"article"}}}